<%# The living style guide's MODALS section, in a browser.

    WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS. style/_modals carries the guide's page-scoped modal
    host and, since the simulator port, two sections whose entire content is
    BUILT BY SCRIPT: the enter/leave controls are generated from the live
    window.ModalAnimations registry at load, and the stack demos drive
    $store.dsModals through timers and advance(). None of that is observable in
    the response bytes — the containers ship EMPTY on purpose, so a String tier
    asserting "the container is there" passes on a page where the script never
    ran and the operator sees four empty boxes.

    WHY THE SHARED HOST IS RENDERED TOO, AND WHY THAT IS NOT SCENERY.
    window.ModalAnimations — the registry the simulator enumerates — is published
    by studio/modals/_host, which every consumer mounts ONCE AT LAYOUT LEVEL.
    /admin/style is always reached through such a layout, so the guide has never
    published that registry itself and does not here either. Without this render
    the registry is UNDEFINED on the lab page, the build falls back to an empty
    table, and the section paints zero controls — over which "the controls match
    the registry" would be vacuously true at 0 == 0. Mounting both hosts is also
    the honest reproduction of the real page: the guide uses a page-scoped store
    PRECISELY BECAUSE the app already owns $store.modals (see style/_modals).

    THE LAB RULE (see E2eLabController): a lab page sets up a partial's LOCALS
    and nothing else. Neither partial here takes any — style/_modals resolves its
    own web3 / leveling gates, and the host is given an EMPTY block because it
    guards its extension point with `yield if block_given?`, which a partial
    rendered inside a layout answers true for whether or not one was passed. %>
<main class="max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4 py-10" x-data>
  <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-6">Style guide — modals</h1>

  <%= render "studio/modals/host" do %><% end %>

  <%= render "style/modals" %>
</main>
